{"name":"Caramel Apple","hex":"#b87a59","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"caramel apple","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dessert or snack made by dipping an apple in liquid heated caramel and often sprinkling with nuts etc., then allowing the caramel to cool and harden."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caramel_apple"}],"entries":[{"title":"Caramel apple","blocks":[{"text":"Caramel apples or toffee apples are whole apples covered in a layer of caramel. They are created by dipping or rolling apples on a stick in hot caramel, sometimes rolling them in nuts or other toppings, and allowing them to cool. When these additional ingredients, such as nut toppings, are added, the caramel apple can be called a taffy apple."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Caramel_Peanut_Candy_Apples_2592px.jpg/330px-Caramel_Peanut_Candy_Apples_2592px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caramel_apple"},{"title":"Caramel","blocks":[{"text":"Caramel is a range of food ingredients made by heating sugars to high temperatures. It is used as a flavoring in puddings and desserts, as a filling in bonbons or candy bars, as a topping for ice cream and custard, and as a colorant commonly used in drinks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Caramel-2.jpg/330px-Caramel-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caramel"},{"title":"Apple","blocks":[{"text":"An apple is the round, edible fruit of an apple tree. Fruit trees of the orchard or domestic apple, the most widely grown in the genus, are cultivated worldwide. The tree originated in Central Asia, where its wild ancestor, Malus sieversii, is still found. Apples have been grown for thousands of years in Eurasia before they were introduced to the American continent by European colonists. Apples have cultural significance in many mythologies and religions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg/330px-Pink_lady_and_cross_section.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Caramels.jpg/500px-Caramels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Caramels.jpg/1280px-Caramels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Caramels.jpg/500px-Caramels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bags of caramels are commonly sold during the fall months in America for making caramel apples.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACaramels.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/The_Childrens_Party_Book_-_Taffy_Apples.png/500px-The_Childrens_Party_Book_-_Taffy_Apples.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/The_Childrens_Party_Book_-_Taffy_Apples.png/500px-The_Childrens_Party_Book_-_Taffy_Apples.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An early candy apple recipe from a 1923 book on children's parties. The recipe uses a brown sugar caramel glaze.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Childrens_Party_Book_-_Taffy_Apples.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Omar-kaare_ja_klontti_web.jpg/500px-Omar-kaare_ja_klontti_web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Omar-kaare_ja_klontti_web.jpg/1280px-Omar-kaare_ja_klontti_web